    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe Luhn algorithm is misplaced. `OwnerMapper` is a MapStruct interface whose `@AfterMapping` methods either do trivial presentation formatting (`addDisplayName`, `addInitials`, `addMembershipNumber`) or delegate a *derived value* to the domain (`addMembershipLevel` \u2192 `owner.getMembershipLevel()`, `addLocality` \u2192 `Region.locality(...)`). The check digit is a derived value computed purely from the domain field `customerCode`, so it belongs with the codebase's established pattern for exactly this shape \u2014 not inlined as a 15-line algorithm inside the mapper.\n\nConcrete findings:\n\n1. **Wrong unit \u2014 computation belongs in the domain.** `Owner` already hosts its derived computations: `getMembershipLevel()` and `getIdentityKey()`. Add an `Owner.getCheckDigit()` (returning `Integer`, null when `customerCode` is null \u2014 mirroring `getMembershipLevel()`'s style and its Javadoc), and have the mapper delegate:\n   ```java\n   @AfterMapping\n   default void addCheckDigit(Owner owner, @MappingTarget OwnerDto ownerDto) {\n       ownerDto.setCheckDigit(owner.getCheckDigit());\n   }\n   ```\n   This makes `addCheckDigit` consistent with its sibling `addMembershipLevel`, keeps the mapper as a thin translation layer, and puts the Luhn logic where it's independently testable \u2014 instead of turning the mapper interface into a catch-all for business algorithms.\n\nThe OpenAPI schema addition for `checkDigit` is fine and idiomatic (matches the surrounding read-only property style).",
